To add navigation links to your `App.js` so that they are visible on all routes, you need to include a `Header` component from `antd` that contains the `Menu` or `MenuOutlined` component. Here is an example of how you can modify your `App.js` to include navigation links:

```jsx
import logo from './ressources/logo.png';
import './App.css';
import React from 'react';
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';
import ExampleComponent from './components/ExampleComponent';
import Layout from './components/Layout';
import Home from './components/Home';
import { Layout, Menu } from 'antd'; // Import Layout and Menu from antd

const { Header } = Layout;

function App() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      <Layout>
        <Header className="header">
          <div className="logo-container">
            <img src={logo} alt="Logo" />
          </div>
          <Menu theme="dark" mode="horizontal" defaultSelectedKeys={['1']}>
            <Menu.Item key="1">Home</Menu.Item>
            <Menu.Item key="2">Test</Menu.Item>
          </Menu>
        </Header>
        <Content className="content">
          <Routes>
            <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
            <Route path="/test" element={<ExampleComponent />} />
          </Routes>
        </Content>
        <Footer className="footer" />
      </Layout>
    </BrowserRouter>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this code:

1. The `Header` component is imported from `antd`.
2. The `Menu` component from `antd` is added within the `Header` component.
3. The `Menu` component has three `Menu.Item` components for "Home" and "Test".
4. The `defaultSelectedKeys` prop in the `Menu` component is set to `['1']` to make "Home" the default selected link.

This should make the navigation visible on all routes. The `Header` component will be rendered at the top of the `Layout`, and the `Menu` will contain the navigation links. The `Content` component will contain the routes, and the `Footer` will be at the bottom.

To create a lambda function in Go that handles file uploads to S3 and operates over HTTP, you'll need to set up an AWS Lambda function with an API Gateway trigger. The Go code should parse the incoming HTTP request, extract the file from the request body, and then upload it to an S3 bucket.

Here's a basic example of what the Go code could look like:

```go
package main

import (
	"bytes"
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"net/http"

	"github.com/aws/aws-lambda-go/events"
	"github.com/aws/aws-lambda-go/lambda"
	"github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/aws"
	"github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/config"
	"github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/s3"
	"github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/s3/types"
)

func main() {
	lambda.Start(uploadFileToS3)
}

func uploadFileToS3(ctx context.Context, request events.APIGatewayProxyRequest) (events.APIGatewayProxyResponse, error) {
	cfg, err := config.LoadDefaultConfig(ctx, config.WithRegion("us-west-2"))
	if err != nil {
		return events.APIGatewayProxyResponse{StatusCode: http.StatusInternalServerError}, err
	}

	s3Client := s3.NewFromConfig(cfg)

	body, err := io.ReadAll(request.Body)
	if err != nil {
		return events.APIGatewayProxyResponse{StatusCode: http.StatusBadRequest}, err
	}

	file, err := createFileFromBytes(body)
	if err != nil {
		return events.APIGatewayProxyResponse{StatusCode: http.StatusInternalServerError}, err
	}

	key := fmt.Sprintf("uploads/%s", file.Name())

	result, err := s3Client.PutObject(ctx, &s3.PutObjectInput{
		Bucket: aws.String("your-bucket-name"),
		Key:    aws.String(key),
		Body:   file,
	})

	if err != nil {
		return events.APIGatewayProxyResponse{StatusCode: http.StatusInternalServerError}, err
	}

	return events.APIGatewayProxyResponse{
		StatusCode: http.StatusOK,
		Body:       result,
	}, nil
}

func createFileFromBytes(body []byte) (io.Reader, error) {
	file, err := createTempFile("upload", body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}

	return file, nil
}

func createTempFile(prefix string, body []byte) (io.Reader, error) {
	tempFile, err := ioutil.TempFile("", prefix)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}

	_, err = tempFile.Write(body)
	if err != nil {
		tempFile.Close()
		os.Remove(tempFile.Name())
		return nil, err
	}

	tempFile.Close()
	return tempFile, nil
}
```

Note: The code above assumes you have set up your AWS credentials and permissions correctly. You will also need to replace `"your-bucket-name"` with the actual name of your S3 bucket.

The above Go code is a simplified version, and you'll need to add proper error handling, logging, and perhaps more sophisticated file handling depending on your use case. Additionally, ensure you have the necessary AWS Lambda and S3 permissions set up in your AWS IAM roles.

Remember, this code is just a starting point, and you'll need to adapt it to your specific requirements.

