model_response:
To focus the first element of your `UntypedFormGroup` when the component starts, you can use the `ViewChild` decorator to access the form control and then call the `focus()` method on it. Here’s how you can do that:

1. Import necessary modules and decorators.
2. Use the `ViewChild` decorator to get a reference to the form control.
3. Call the `focus()` method in the `ngOnInit` lifecycle hook.

Here's the complete code:

```typescript
import { Component, OnInit, ViewChild, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';
import { UntypedFormGroup, UntypedFormControl, Validators } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-component',
  templateUrl: './my-component.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./my-component.component.css']
})
export class MyComponent implements OnInit {
  form: UntypedFormGroup = new UntypedFormGroup({
    fieldname: new UntypedFormControl("", Validators.required)
  });

  @ViewChild('fieldname', { static: true }) fieldname: ElementRef;

  ngOnInit() {
    this.form.get('fieldname').valueChanges.pipe(
      first() // Wait for the first value change
    ).subscribe(() => {
      this.form.get('fieldname').focus();
    });
  }
}
```

In your HTML template, make sure to have an element with the reference id `fieldname`:

```html
<input #fieldname [formGroup]="form" type="text">
```

This will ensure that the first element of your form is focused when the component starts. The `first()` operator from RxJS ensures that we only focus the first time the value changes, which in this case, happens right when the component initializes.

