To achieve this in React, you can use the `useEffect` hook along with `useRef` and the `getBoundingClientRect` method to determine if the element's bottom is visible in the window. Here's an example of how you can implement this:

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';

const ScrollToBottom = ({ children }) => {
  const ref = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const element = ref.current;
    if (element) {
      const elementRect = element.getBoundingClientRect();
      const windowHeight = window.innerHeight;

      // Check if the bottom of the element is not visible in the window
      if (elementRect.bottom > windowHeight) {
        element.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'end' });
      }
    }
  }, []);

  return <div ref={ref}>{children}</div>;
};

export default ScrollToBottom;
```

In this example, `ScrollToBottom` is a reusable React component that takes children as a prop. The `ref` is used to get a reference to the DOM element. The `useEffect` hook is used to perform side effects, and it runs once after the initial render.

Inside the `useEffect`, we get the element's bounding rectangle using `getBoundingClientRect` and compare its bottom position to the window's inner height. If the bottom of the element is not visible in the window, we call `scrollIntoView` with the specified options to scroll the element into view smoothly.

You can use this `ScrollToBottom` component in your application to automatically scroll to the bottom of a specified element if its bottom is not visible in the window.

Sure, I can help you create a simple Flutter website. However, Flutter is primarily used for mobile and desktop app development, not web development. If you want to create a web application using Flutter, you can use the `flutter_web` library. Below is a simple example of how to create a basic Flutter web application:

1. First, make sure you have Flutter installed on your machine. You can find the installation guide here: https://flutter.dev/docs/get-started/install

2. Create a new Flutter project:
   ```
   flutter create my_flutter_web
   cd my_flutter_web
   ```

3. Update the `pubspec.yaml` file to include the `flutter_web` dependency:
   ```yaml
   dependencies:
     flutter:
       sdk: flutter
     flutter_web: ^0.4.0
   ```

4. Run `flutter pub get` to install the dependencies.

5. Modify the `main.dart` file to create a simple Flutter web application:
   ```dart
   import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
   import 'package:flutter_web/flutter_web.dart';

   void main() => runApp(MyApp());

   class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
     @override
     Widget build(BuildContext context) {
       return MaterialApp(
         title: 'Flutter Web App',
         theme: ThemeData(
           primarySwatch: Colors.blue,
         ),
         home: Scaffold(
           appBar: AppBar(
             title: Text('Flutter Web App'),
           ),
           body: Center(
             child: Text('Hello, Flutter Web!'),
           ),
         ),
       );
     }
   }
   ```

6. Run the Flutter web application:
   ```
   flutter run -d chrome --web-renderer html
   ```

This will launch your Flutter web application in a Chrome browser. You can now navigate to the provided URL to see your simple Flutter web application in action. You can further customize and expand this application to suit your needs.

